I fixed this in `packages/compiler-sfc/src/style/pluginScoped.ts`. Declarations sitting directly inside a nested `@media`, `@container` or other at-rule now get wrapped in `&[data-v-…]`, just like the outer rule's declarations already were.

**Cause:** in a scoped style, when a rule like `.foo` has a nested rule (here, the empty `.bar {}`), Vue moves `.foo`'s own declarations into an `&[data-v-…]` block. That block carries the scope attribute, so it's more specific than plain `.foo`. Declarations directly inside a nested at-rule were left as they were, so they applied with `.foo`'s lower specificity and lost to `background-color: red`.

**Fix:** I moved the wrapping logic into a new helper, `extractAndWrapNodes`. It now runs on the rule itself and on each at-rule directly inside it. As before, this only happens when the rule has nested rules and isn't a `:deep` rule.

Given the issue's case plus a nested `.baz` rule inside the `@media`, the output is now:

```css
.foo {
&[data-v-test] { background-color: red }
.bar[data-v-test] {}
@media only screen and (max-width: 800px) {
&[data-v-test] { background-color: green }
.baz[data-v-test] { color: red }
}
}
```

I checked this with a throwaway test, since deleted. The existing `compileStyle` tests still pass (25 of 25). I didn't add a permanent test.

One small behaviour change: comments are now moved into the `&` block along with the declarations, instead of staying where they were. I did this so a comment stays with the declarations it describes, but it does change the output slightly when comments are present.

